    <string name="about_title">How sentiment is scored</string>
    <string name="about_body">Every post and headline is scored from −1 (very negative) to +1 (very positive) with VADER, a sentiment model built for social media. It understands negation (\"not good\"), intensity (\"very good\"), emphasis (CAPS, !!!) and contrast (\"good, but…\"). A market lexicon on top adds trading slang, emoji and phrases such as \"all-time high\", and stops words like \"shares\" or \"interest\" from counting as positive. When StockTwits authors tag a post Bullish or Bearish, that tag is blended in.\n\nThe overall score gives more weight to recent posts and to posts with more engagement, and leans toward neutral when there are only a few posts. The score out of 100 maps −1…+1 onto 0…100, so 50 is neutral.\n\nSources: StockTwits, Bluesky, Reddit and Google News. Prices from Yahoo Finance; trending coins from CoinGecko. This is not financial advice.</string>
